# ACT ONE

## The Shop

FADE IN:

EXT. DESERT HIGHWAY - DAWN

= June finds the shop she has been looking for.

A ribbon of asphalt cuts through saguaros. Heat shimmer already rising. A battered pickup rattles toward a single building on the horizon: a bookbinder's shop with a hand-painted sign. *LAST PAGE BINDERY.*

INT. LAST PAGE BINDERY - CONTINUOUS

Dust hangs in slanted light. Shelves bow under the weight of unfinished books -- spines without titles, covers without names.

JUNE ALVAREZ (30s, sunburned, a canvas bag held like a heartbeat) steps inside. A bell rings above the door.

From the back, OTIS PRUITT (70s, ink on every finger) looks up from a press.

OTIS
We're closed.

JUNE
Your sign says open.

OTIS
(not looking)
The sign is an optimist.

June sets the bag on the counter. Pulls out a manuscript, swollen with water damage, held together with twine.

JUNE
My father wrote this. Forty years. He never finished the last page.

Otis wipes his hands. Lifts the cover with one finger. Reads. Something in his face goes still.

OTIS
Where did you get this?

JUNE
I told you. My father.

OTIS
Your father was Raymond Alvarez.

JUNE
How do you know that?

OTIS
Because the last page is here.

He points to a locked cabinet behind him. A brass key hangs on a nail above it, just out of reach.

JUNE ^
You've had it this whole time?

OTIS ^
I've had it since before you were born.

CUT TO:

EXT. LAST PAGE BINDERY - LATER

The sun is high now. June sits on the tailgate of the pickup, the manuscript in her lap. Otis comes out with two glasses of water and hands her one.

OTIS
He came here in nineteen eighty-six. Asked me to bind the book when it was done. Said he'd bring the ending the next week.

JUNE
And?

OTIS
He brought the ending. Then he asked me to keep it. Said if anyone ever came looking for it, it meant the book was finished without him. That the story had found its own way. That I should give them the page only if they could tell me what it said without reading it. He was a strange man. A good one. But strange. I never understood what he meant until this morning, when I saw you standing in my door holding that bag the way he used to hold it.

June stares at the horizon.

JUNE
(quietly)
I think I know what it says.

[[Consider moving this reveal to the end of Act Two.]]

SMASH CUT TO:

INT. JUNE'S CHILDHOOD HOME - NIGHT (FLASHBACK)

A small kitchen. A typewriter on the table. RAYMOND ALVAREZ (40s) types by lamplight. Young JUNE (8) watches from the doorway in pajamas.

RAYMOND
Can't sleep?

YOUNG JUNE
How does it end?

RAYMOND
(smiling)
That's the trick, mija. The ending isn't mine to write.

> THE END <
